  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 110 AM EDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nLight showers have ended across the area. The drying and cooling \ntrend is set to begin. A closed low just off of the mid-Atlantic \ncoast will slowly drift north as upper level ridging moves in from\nthe west, centering over the middle of the country into the \nweekend and the first full week of October. Under the upper low, \ncoastal storm impacts are being felt across much of the Atlantic \ncoast. Our area will be situated under high pressure with no \nprecipitation in the forecast until maybe the end of the forecast \nperiod when another closed upper low/shortwave barrels east from \nCA and the desert SW. Of course, there are discrepancies this far \nout as to what will happen and when.\n\nUnder lower heights aloft from the coastal system and surface high \npressure, temperatures will be running close to or just below normal \nfor end of September and early October. A very welcomed thing to \nthose tired of daily storms and record setting hot temperatures. Far \nnortheastern parts of the CWA this morning may see lows in the 40s \nfor the first time in a while. Drier air will continue filtering in, \nwhich will allow much cooler temperatures tomorrow morning and \nespecially Sunday morning. 40s for lows could sink as far south as \nthe I-40 corridor, excluding the Southern Appalachians. As ridging \nis allowed to edge a bit more east following the exit of the coastal \nupper low early next week, as well as surface high pressure moving \nto our east, warmer temperatures will begin increasing each day \nstarting on Monday. Valley temperatures at the end of the forecast \nperiod will return to the mid to upper 80s and possibly more humid \nconditions.",
